===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A low-circulation, non-commercial publication of original or appropriated texts and images, especially one of minority interest.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2005|author=Kim Cooper|chapter=Mimeos and Cut-Out Bins|chapterurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=jFKsR0K82hYC&pg=PT14|editor=David Smay|title=Lost in the Grooves: Scram’s Capricious Guide to the Music You Missed|publisher=Routledge|isbn=9781135879211|passage='''Zines''' contributed to an evolving critical language that would ultimately take two paths: into the gut or to the academy. The most compelling '''zines''' fused the two.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2008|author=Samantha Holland|title=Remote Relationships in a Small World|publisher=Peter Lang|isbn=9780820486291|page=21|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=NBVGKaM8VEEC&pg=PA21#v=onepage&q&f=false|passage=The feminist '''zine''' community is not located in place but it geographically dispersed, constituting a connected flow of communicative practices, spaces, texts, technologies, bodies, and utterances.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2013|author=Barbara J. Guzzetti|author2=Thomas W. Bean|title=Adolescent Literacies and the Gendered Self: (Re)Constructing Identities through Multimodal Literacy Practices|publisher=Routledge|isbn=9780415636186|page=58|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=iXjA4WJnItEC&pg=PA58|passage=I conducted a content analysis of the '''zines''' I collected by using techniques of thematic analysis (Patton, 1990). I read and reread each of the '''zines’''' contents. I annotated the prose, cartoons, poetry, and narratives in the '''zines''' by noting key words that signaled topics and assigning codes and subcodes that were later collapsed to form categories.}}

